  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Village song repertoire of undivided Punjab — tappe, boliyan, mahiya, dholi and jugni forms sung at weddings, harvests and giddha circles, carried through the 20th century by Yamla Jatt, Asa Singh Mastana and Surinder Kaur and archived by the Punjabi Academy — mostly free or loosely metric couplets over tumbi, algoza and dholak, with handclaps for giddha, sung in a strained high tessitura with long held vowels and abrupt terminal drops, harmonium entering only in recorded/broadcast settings.",
  "recipe": "classic South Asian early-1940s punjabi folk, field Work, south Asian branch, south Asian domestic rhythm, south Asian carnival processional, indian bard ballad poetry bhojpuri lok geet himalayan folk. thick, breath veiled tone, slow rate, interlocked note trading. steel single string canonical Punjabi ornamented tumbi, algoza, dholak, hearth tongs driving groove chimta, vocal male straight harmonium indian, vocal imitating gut 3 main playing strings sarangi. punjab village bhangra dhol outdoor. small-diaphragm pencil condenser, portable field-recorder pre. tape 15ips.",
